# Analysis Notes

The MAX30102 temperature experiment data were analyzed using Google Sheets.

Each CSV file contained three columns:

csv
time_ms,red,ir
Because the sensor readings were still stabilizing immediately after finger placement, the first 10 seconds of each recording were excluded from the summary statistics.
In Google Sheets, the cutoff was implemented using the time_ms column. Since the data started at row 5 in the sheet, the cutoff condition was:

A5:A >= A5 + 10000
For example, the mean red value after excluding the first 10 seconds was calculated using:
=AVERAGE(FILTER(B5:B,A5:A>=A5+10000))
The same method was used for the red and IR columns. The following summary statistics were calculated:
mean red
sample standard deviation of red
range of red
mean IR
sample standard deviation of IR
range of IR
The formulas used were:
=AVERAGE(FILTER(B5:B,A5:A>=A5+10000))
=STDEV.S(FILTER(B5:B,A5:A>=A5+10000))
=MAX(FILTER(B5:B,A5:A>=A5+10000))-MIN(FILTER(B5:B,A5:A>=A5+10000))
=AVERAGE(FILTER(C5:C,A5:A>=A5+10000))
=STDEV.S(FILTER(C5:C,A5:A>=A5+10000))
=MAX(FILTER(C5:C,A5:A>=A5+10000))-MIN(FILTER(C5:C,A5:A>=A5+10000))

Here, column A was time_ms, column B was red, and column C was ir.
